.TH KLVTOOL-DIAGNOSE 1 "1970-01-01" "klvtool dev" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
klvtool\-diagnose \- Run the full diagnostic pipeline: health check, transport inspect, video analysis, and KLV decode.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
klvtool diagnose \-\-input <path\-or\-url> [\-\-view auto|pretty|raw] [\-\-repair\-stripped\-ul] [\-\-duration <dur>] [\-\-idle\-timeout <dur>] [\-\-max\-packets <N>] [\-\-header "K: V"] [\-\-iface <name>]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Run a one\-shot diagnostic pipeline against a single MPEG\-TS file: backend health check → transport inspection → H.264 video bitstream analysis (non\-fatal) → KLV decode of every likely metadata PID.
.PP
This is the right entry point for triaging a fresh mission file. The output is a single consolidated report ending in either a clean run or a `Stopped at: <stage>` line that names the failing stage. Video analysis reports a playability verdict (PLAYABLE / STALLS_IN_MSE / DEGRADED) for any H.264 stream — useful for catching missing IDR frames that prevent hls.js / MSE playback.
.PP
\-\-input also accepts URLs (udp://, tcp://, http(s)://, rtsp://, srt://, or '\-' for stdin). For live sources, diagnose runs a bounded inspect window and one decode pass per candidate PID; the video\-analysis stage is skipped because it requires a seekable file (capture with `klvtool record` and re\-run for video verdicts).
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\fR \fI(default 0s)\fR
stop after this wall\-clock duration (e.g. 30s, 5m, 1h); 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-header\fR
extra HTTP request header in 'Key: Value' form (repeatable; e.g. \-header "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"). HTTPS only — RTSP servers must embed credentials in the URL until token\-auth is wired up.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\-timeout\fR \fI(default 0s)\fR
stop if no inbound bytes are observed for this duration; 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-iface\fR
egress network interface for UDP multicast joins (Linux: device name like eth0, or a local IP)
.TP
\fB\-\-input\fR
MPEG\-TS input path or URL (file://, udp://, tcp://, http(s)://, rtsp://, srt://, or '\-' for stdin)
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-packets\fR
stop after this many TS packets are observed; 0 disables
.TP
\fB\-\-record\fR
tee inbound source bytes to this file (works for any \-\-input, including a regular file)
.TP
\fB\-\-record\-overwrite\fR
allow \-\-record to overwrite an existing file (default refuses; honors O_CREATE|O_EXCL semantics)
.TP
\fB\-\-repair\-stripped\-ul\fR
re\-attach the 5\-byte SMPTE category prefix that some ffmpeg builds strip from KLV data streams
.TP
\fB\-\-view\fR \fI(default auto)\fR
presentation mode: auto, pretty, or raw
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Triage a fresh mission file end to end
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool diagnose --input mission.ts
.fi
.RE
.PP
Quick health check of a live UDP feed (10 seconds inspect + per\-PID decode)
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool diagnose --input udp://239.0.0.1:5000 --duration 10s
.fi
.RE
.SH "TROUBLESHOOTING"
.SS `Stopped at: health check`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
ffmpeg or ffprobe not found on PATH; the extraction backend cannot start.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Install ffmpeg and re\-run. Confirm with `klvtool doctor`. The doctor output prints platform\-specific install steps.
.SS `Stopped at: inspect`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
MPEG\-TS read or parse failure: the file is unreadable, severely truncated, or not actually a TS stream.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Re\-pull the file from the source if possible. Otherwise run `klvtool inspect \-\-input <file>` directly to see the underlying error and check whether the file has a valid TS sync byte and program map.
.SS `Stopped at: decode`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
The metadata stream is structurally invalid for the auto\-detected MISB spec, or transport corruption is severe enough to break KLV framing on every recovery attempt.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Run `klvtool decode \-\-input <file> \-\-pid <PID> \-\-format text` to see per\-packet diagnostics. If decode fails everywhere, run `klvtool extract` then `klvtool packetize \-\-mode best\-effort` to surface byte offsets where the wire format diverges, and escalate to platform / sensor engineering with that context.
.SS `No likely metadata streams found.`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
The transport is healthy but the program map advertises no KLV / data PIDs. Either the sensor was not configured to emit metadata for this capture, or the metadata is muxed under a stream type klvtool does not yet recognize.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Confirm the platform was emitting KLV during this mission (sensor config, mission profile). Run `klvtool inspect \-\-input <file>` to see the full PID inventory and consider running `klvtool decode \-\-input <file>` (no \-\-pid) to attempt decode across every PID.
.SS Decode succeeds but reports many error diagnostics
\fBLikely cause:\fR
Likely a non\-compliant or pre\-1.x sensor: structural validation against ST 0601 fails on individual tags but framing is intact.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Inspect the diagnostic codes (`tag_decode_error`, `unknown_tag`, `validation_failed`). If a vendor\-specific spec is in use, try `\-\-schema <urn>` to override auto\-detection. If results are still poor, capture an offending packet's bytes via `\-\-raw` and escalate with the diagnostic context.
.SS Every decoded packet reports `unknown_spec` and a `stripped_ul_prefix` warning fires
\fBLikely cause:\fR
Some ffmpeg builds strip the first 5 bytes (the SMPTE Groups category prefix `06 0e 2b 34 02`) from KLV packets when extracting via `\-c copy \-f data`. The remaining 11\-byte UL stub doesn't match any registered MISB spec, so every packet is rejected.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Re\-run with `\-\-repair\-stripped\-ul` to re\-attach the prefix in memory before decode. If you prefer to fix upstream, use an ffmpeg build that preserves the full UL or pre\-process the .ts so the data PID is delivered with intact Universal Labels.
.SS Video verdict `STALLS_IN_MSE`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
The H.264 stream is missing IDR frames or SPS/PPS — common with some encoder configurations. The metadata path is unaffected; only browser\-based MSE playback (hls.js, video.js) stalls.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
If you only need metadata, ignore the verdict — KLV decode is independent. For playback, re\-encode with libx264 forcing an IDR every keyframe; the diagnose output prints a ready\-to\-use `ffmpeg ... libx264 \-g 60 \-keyint_min 60 \-sc_threshold 0` command in the FixHint when applicable.
.SS Video verdict `DEGRADED`
\fBLikely cause:\fR
IDRs present but PES PTS deltas indicate dropped frames in transit.
.PP
\fBAction:\fR
Treat as a transport\-quality signal. If lossy delivery is the norm for this feed, the file is still usable for analysis; if not, re\-pull or investigate the upstream link.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
pipeline completed (with or without warnings)
.TP
\fB1\fR
a stage failed (health check, inspect, or decode); see `Stopped at: <stage>` for which
.TP
\fB2\fR
invalid usage
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
disable ANSI color in pretty output
.SH "REQUIRED TOOLS"
ffmpeg, ffprobe
.SH "SEE ALSO"
